      body: 'Twelve-year-old Malik had a school project on family history, and he figured it would take an afternoon. He would ask his great-grandmother, Mother Ruth, two questions, write a page, and be done. Mother Ruth was ninety-one and lived with his family in Charlotte. When he asked where the family came from, she did not answer. She sent him to her room for a Nike shoebox under the bed. Inside were funeral programs going back to 1952, a deed with a torn corner, a church anniversary booklet from Marlboro County, South Carolina, a ration book, and a photograph of a stern man in a high collar. “That’s my granddaddy, Isaac,” she said. “He was born free, but his daddy wasn’t.” Malik spent the whole weekend at the kitchen table. His uncle Dwayne, who had not spoken to Malik’s mother in two years over the house after Grandpa died, came by to drop off a plate, saw the papers spread out, and sat down. He knew things nobody else did: which cousin had moved to Detroit, where the old church cemetery stood. By Sunday night Uncle Dwayne and Malik’s mother were bent over the same deed, arguing about a date, but laughing. Malik’s teacher had told the class that historians do not trust a story until they check it. So Malik checked: the deed matched a county record online, and the church booklet matched the cemetery list. He found one thing that did not match, a story that Isaac had owned two hundred acres, and he wrote in his report that he could only prove forty. Mother Ruth laughed until she coughed. “Forty is plenty,” she said. “Forty is true.” At the bottom of the report Malik wrote the verse his pastor had preached that month: "My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge" (Hosea 4:6). Under it, in his own handwriting: Not this family. Not anymore.',
