  bigIdea:
    'Williams opens Part I of his book with an epigraph he labels a Sumer legend: a traveller asks what became of the Black people of Sumer, and an old man answers, "They lost their history, so they died." From that warning he builds his question, how a highly advanced Black civilization was so completely destroyed, and his method: world history, Africa’s own treasures studied in Europe’s museums, the colonial powers’ own documents, and two years of oral history in the field, with no record accepted without verification. The epigraph itself carries no source and its claim about Sumer is not the consensus; the warning it carries is the Word’s own.',
