      title: 'The History Team at Mount Pisgah',
      body: 'When Pastor Okafor announced that Mount Pisgah Missionary Baptist would turn one hundred, the anniversary committee wanted a booklet by spring. Deacon Lorraine Mims volunteered to write the history, and everyone assumed she would do it alone, the way she did everything. By February she was exhausted. The church minutes from the 1940s were in a basement box with water damage. Half the founding families had moved away. And the story everyone told, that the first building was raised in a single week by men who came straight from the mill, did not match the one deed she found. At Bible study she confessed she was stuck. Brother Terrence, who worked at the county library, offered to search the newspaper archive. Sister Amara, a nursing student, knew how to record interviews on her phone. Two teenagers from the youth group scanned every photograph. Old Mother Pinckney, who could not walk the stairs anymore, sat in the fellowship hall and told stories while Amara recorded. They made a rule: nothing goes in the booklet unless two sources agree, and anything only remembered gets labeled as remembered. The one-week building story turned out to be closer to five months, with the men working after their mill shifts by lantern light. Nobody was disappointed. Five months of lantern nights sounded holier than one week. Terrence found a 1926 newspaper notice of the first baptism in the creek, with nineteen names. Eleven of those families still sat in the pews. At the anniversary, the booklet’s first page carried a verse Lorraine had chosen: "ask thy father, and he will shew thee; thy elders, and they will tell thee" (Deuteronomy 32:7). Under it she wrote, in plain letters: Gathered by a team, checked twice, given to our children.',
