      tone: 'solemn',
      title: 'The Grandson Who Did Not Know',
      body: 'Raymond Fields buried his father on a Tuesday and found out who his father was on Wednesday. At the repast, an older man Raymond had never met took his hand and said, “Your daddy integrated the shipyard in sixty-three. They put sugar in his gas tank every week for a year. He never missed a shift.” Raymond had not known. His father had never said a word about it. He had only said, again and again, “Keep your head down and do your work.” Raymond had raised his own sons on the same sentence, and his sons had heard it as shame. One of them, Jaylen, nineteen, had told Raymond at Christmas that their family had never done anything worth talking about. On Wednesday Raymond drove to the union hall and asked for records. A retired secretary found a folder with his father’s name, a grievance letter, and a photograph of six Black welders standing in front of a hull, unsmiling, arms folded. Raymond sat in his truck with that photograph for an hour. He thought about the silence that had come down three generations, how a man’s courage had shrunk into a rule about keeping your head down, and how his grandson might grow up knowing nothing at all. He thought of a verse he had read at the funeral without really hearing it: "That the generation to come might know them, even the children which should be born; who should arise and declare them to their children:" (Psalms 78:6) That night he called Jaylen and asked him to come over. He laid the photograph on the kitchen table. He told him everything the old man had said, and then he said what he had learned from the folder, and he was careful to say which was which. Jaylen did not speak for a long time. Then he asked if there were other photographs. There were. They started looking together.',
