    'In his chapter titled Ethiopia’s Oldest Daughter: Egypt, Williams argues that Egypt was first the north-eastern region of an ancient Ethiopian (Nile Valley) civilization, that its wealth drew peoples from Asia and later Europe into the delta, and that wherever the newcomers gained control the Blacks were pushed to the bottom of the social, economic and political ladder. He calls the later teaching of Egypt as something other than African the great deception. Established: Egypt was an indigenous African Nile Valley civilization, Kush was a great power whose kings ruled Egypt, and the Word names Cush and Mizraim as brothers. Contested: an Ethiopian empire that contained Egypt at its founding, and an all-black Egypt turned lighter by a deliberate policy.',
