      title: 'The Map on the Fellowship Hall Wall',
      body: 'Sister Gwendolyn Ashby had taught the fourth-grade Sunday school class at Shiloh for twenty-two years, and every year she pinned the same faded map of the Bible lands on the fellowship hall wall. Every year it stopped at the bottom edge of Egypt, as though the world ended there. This year her grandson DeShawn, home from his first semester of college, looked at it and asked, “Grandma, where’s Ethiopia?” She did not know what to say. So that Sunday she let him teach. He brought a new map that showed the whole Nile, all the way south past the cataracts. He read the class the table of nations: "And the sons of Ham; Cush, and Mizraim, and Phut, and Canaan." (Genesis 10:6) Then he pointed at the map. “Cush is Ethiopia,” he told them, “and Mizraim is Egypt. Brothers.” Then he gave each child a card with a name: Tirhakah, Ebedmelech, the Ethiopian treasurer, Moses’ wife from Cush. Each child had to find the verse and tell the story. A girl named Precious read how Ebedmelech pulled Jeremiah out of the mud with old rags and ropes, and she acted it out with the jump rope from the closet. A boy named Isaiah, very serious, asked whether the pharaohs looked like him. DeShawn told the truth: “Some of them did. Egypt was an African kingdom, and people there came in a lot of shades. The Bible doesn’t sort people by color the way we do. It says we come from one blood.” Isaiah thought about that and nodded. At the end Sister Ashby took the old map down. She did not throw it away. She folded it and put it in her Bible, as a reminder of how much a map can leave out, and she pinned the new one where every child could see the whole river.',
