      tone: 'solemn',
      title: 'What Grandmother Said at the Wedding',
      body: 'When Keisha announced she was marrying Daniel, whose mother was Black and whose father was white, her uncle Lonnie refused to come. He had read Chancellor Williams as a young man in the seventies, carried The Destruction of Black Civilization like a second Bible, and he quoted to anyone who would listen the line about the Blacks being pushed to the bottom wherever the mixed and the newcomers took control. “History tells you what happens,” he said. Keisha cried for a week. Her grandmother, Mother Hattie Coleman, ninety, said nothing until the rehearsal dinner. Then she asked Lonnie to drive her home, and on the way she asked him to pull over. “I lived through Jim Crow,” she said. “I know what it is to be pushed to the bottom. Your Dr. Williams was right about a lot. But you are using him to do the same thing that was done to us: judging a man by his blood.” Lonnie said nothing. She opened her small Bible to Numbers and read it in the dark of the car by the dome light: "And Miriam and Aaron spake against Moses because of the Ethiopian woman whom he had married: for he had married an Ethiopian woman." (Numbers 12:1) She let the words sit a moment, then turned to him. “Yahweh did not take Miriam’s side,” she said. “He took the side of that Ethiopian woman. Don’t you be Miriam at your niece’s wedding.” Lonnie came. He sat in the back row with his arms folded, and he did not dance. But when the pastor read that Yahweh had made of one blood all nations of men, he unfolded his arms. Months later, he gave Daniel his worn copy of Williams’s book, with a note inside the cover: Read it. It is true about a lot. It is not the last word. The Word is.',
