  bigIdea:
    'What sets Williams apart is that he refused to blame outsiders alone. Writing of the fall of the Mwanamutapa empire, he says that to make Asians and Europeans solely or mainly responsible would ignore "the principal internal factor: disunity," for the conquerors did not have to divide and conquer when the people were already divided. He traces the disunity to a political tradition that kept splintering into small states and languages, and to centralization that broke faith with the people. Yet he also recovers what was good: customary laws that guarded equal protection, a home, land, aid in trouble, petition, and a fair hearing. The Word confirms the diagnosis and supplies the cure.',
