      body: 'The Harmon Street Block Club had fourteen members and three opinions about everything. When the city announced a grant for neighborhood gardens, Mr. Bernard wanted vegetables, Ms. Tasha wanted a playground corner, and the Reverend Pruitt wanted a memorial bench for the young men the street had lost. By the second meeting, people were talking about starting their own clubs. Ms. Tasha already had a name for hers. Then eighty-year-old Miss Odessa, who rarely spoke, raised her cane. “When I came up from Georgia,” she said, “we had one church, one burial society and one grocery co-op, and we survived things you children never saw. You know why? Nobody could leave. There wasn’t anywhere to go.” Everyone laughed, but she was not finished. “Now everybody can leave. So you have to choose to stay.” She asked Reverend Pruitt to read something. He opened his phone and read the words of Jesus: "every city or house divided against itself shall not stand" (Matthew 12:25). Then they did something new. Instead of voting, they each wrote down what they needed most. Mr. Bernard admitted he mostly wanted his grandsons to learn to grow something. Ms. Tasha admitted she mostly wanted the little ones off the street. The Reverend wanted the boys remembered. They designed one garden: raised vegetable beds the teenagers would tend, a play corner by the fence, and a bench in the middle with the names of the young men carved into it. The grant came through because the city liked a block that agreed. On opening day, Miss Odessa sat on the memorial bench and watched little ones play while teenagers argued about tomatoes, and she said it was the best argument she had heard in years.',
