      title: 'The Credit Union in the Church Basement',
      body: 'For years the men’s fellowship at St. Paul AME talked about starting something. Every few months someone would stand up with a big idea: a grocery store, a bank, a school. Each idea belonged to the man who proposed it, and when the others did not fall in line, he would leave hurt. Then Brother Calvin Tate, a retired postal supervisor who had read Chancellor Williams in his thirties, tried something different. He did not bring a plan. He brought a notebook. For three months he asked every member of the church one question: “What would make the biggest difference for your family?” He wrote down every answer. The answer that came up most was not a store or a school. It was small loans: car repairs, security deposits, a first semester’s books, without the payday lenders on Main Street. So the fellowship studied. Two retired bankers taught a class. A young lawyer in the congregation looked up the rules for a community credit union. They wrote the plan down and posted it on the bulletin board with a sign: “This is a proposal. Mark it up.” People did. A single mother added a savings program for children. The youth group asked for a financial literacy class. Pastor Johnson preached that month from Habakkuk: "Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it" (Habakkuk 2:2). Two years later the credit union opened in the church basement with three hundred members. Brother Calvin refused to be its president. “It was never my plan,” he said. “It was ours.” The first loan went to a young man who needed a transmission so he could keep his job driving across town.',
